transitive_in_run_suppliers.interface:
For a finite $+1$-boundary configuration of the nearest-neighbor Ising model on a square box in $\mathbb Z^2$, draw a dual edge across each nearest-neighbor bond whose endpoint spins disagree. The resulting finite dual graph has even degree at every vertex and decomposes into edge-disjoint simple dual cycles. For each such cycle $\gamma$, define the inside of a primal site by the parity of intersections of the horizontal ray from that site to $+\infty$ with $\gamma$. Then the inside is finite and contained in the variable box, and two neighboring primal sites have different inside parity exactly when their bond is crossed by $\gamma$. Consequently, if the spin at the origin is $-1$, at least one cycle in the decomposition has the origin inside.

The schematic below places primal sites at integer-coordinate intersections and dual vertices at pairs of half-integers. Each thick dual segment crosses the perpendicular primal bond at its midpoint; the dashed ray from the origin crosses the displayed cycle once. The center spin is negative and the surrounding spins are positive. This is one local picture of the parity convention, not a proof of the separation or cycle-decomposition claims.

